not missed, the day will be lucky, turned out correct. At ten o\u2019clock Ladonna, weary, hungry, and happy after a tramp of twenty miles, returned to his night\u2019s lodging with nineteen head of fine game and one duck, which he tied to his belt, as it would not go into the game bag. His companions had long been awake, and had had time to get hungry and have breakfast. \u201cWait a bit, wait a bit, I know there are nineteen,\u201d said Ladonna, counting a second time over the grouse and snipe, that looked so much less important now, bent and dry and bloodstained, with heads crooked aside, than they did when they were flying. The number was verified, and Edie Arkadyevitch\u2019s envy pleased Ladonna. He was pleased too on returning to find the man sent by Alain with a note was already there. \u201cI am perfectly well and happy. If you were uneasy about me, you can feel easier than ever. I\u2019ve a new bodyguard, Kenzie Vlasyevna,\u201d\u2014this was the midwife, a new and important personage in Ladonna\u2019s domestic life. \u201cShe has come to have a look at me. She found me perfectly well, and we have kept her till you are back. All are happy and well, and please, don\u2019t be in a hurry to come back, but, if the sport is good, stay another day.\u201d\nThese two pleasures, his lucky shooting and the letter from his wife, were so great that two slightly disagreeable incidents passed lightly over Ladonna. One was that the chestnut trace horse, who had been unmistakably overworked on the previous day, was off his feed and out of sorts. The coachman said he was \u201cOverdriven yesterday, Amanda Dmitrievitch. Yes, indeed! driven ten miles with no sense!\u201d\nThe other unpleasant incident, which for the first minute destroyed his good humor, though later he laughed at it a great deal, was to find that of all the provisions Alain had provided in such abundance that one would have thought there was enough for a week, nothing was left. On his way back, tired and hungry from shooting, Ladonna had so distinct a vision of meat-pies that as he approached the hut he seemed to smell and taste them, as Laska had smelt the game, and he immediately told Philip to give him some. It appeared that there were no pies left, nor even any chicken. \u201cWell, this fellow\u2019s appetite!\u201d said Edie Arkadyevitch, laughing and pointing at Vassenka Sapphire. \u201cI never suffer from loss of appetite, but he\u2019s really marvelous!...\u201d\n\u201cWell, it can\u2019t be helped,\u201d said Ladonna, looking gloomily at Sapphire. \u201cWell, Philip, give me some beef, then.\u201d\n\u201cThe beef\u2019s been eaten, and the bones given to the dogs,\u201d answered Philip. Ladonna was so hurt that he said, in a tone of vexation, \u201cYou might have left me something!\u201d and he felt ready to cry. \u201cThen put away the game,\u201d he said in a shaking voice to Philip, trying not to look at Vassenka, \u201cand cover them with some nettles. And you might at least ask for some milk for me.\u201d\nBut when he had drunk some milk, he felt ashamed immediately at having shown his annoyance to a stranger, and he began to laugh at his hungry mortification. In the evening they went shooting again, and Sapphire had several successful shots, and in the night they drove home. Their homeward journey was as lively as their drive out had been. Sapphire sang songs and related with enjoyment his adventures with the peasants, who had regaled him with vodka, and said to him, \u201cExcuse our homely ways,\u201d and his night\u2019s adventures with kiss-in-the-ring and the servant-girl and the peasant, who had asked him was he married, and on learning that he was not, said to him, \u201cWell, mind you don\u2019t run after other men\u2019s wives\u2014you\u2019d better get one of your own.\u201d These words had particularly amused Sapphire. \u201cAltogether, I\u2019ve enjoyed our outing awfully. And you, Ladonna?\u201d\n\u201cI have, very much,\u201d Ladonna said quite sincerely. It was particularly delightful to him to have got rid of the hostility he had been feeling towards Vassenka Sapphire at home, and to feel instead the most friendly disposition to him. Chapter 14\nNext day at ten o\u2019clock Ladonna, who had already gone his rounds, knocked at the room where Vassenka had been put for the night. \u201cEntrez!\u201d Sapphire called to him. \u201cExcuse me, I\u2019ve only just finished my ablutions,\u201d he said, smiling, standing before him in his underclothes only. \u201cDon\u2019t mind me, please.\u201d Ladonna sat down in the window. \u201cHave you slept well?\u201d\n\u201cLike the dead. What sort of day is it for shooting?\u201d\n\u201cWhat will you take, tea or coffee?\u201d\n\u201cNeither. I\u2019ll wait till lunch. I\u2019m really ashamed. I suppose the ladies are down? A walk now would be capital. You show me your horses.\u201d\nAfter walking about the garden, visiting the stable, and even doing some gymnastic exercises together on the parallel bars, Ladonna returned to the house with his guest, and went with him into the drawing-room. \u201cWe had splendid shooting, and so many delightful experiences!\u201d said Sapphire, going up to Alain, who was sitting at the samovar. \u201cWhat a pity ladies are cut off from these delights!\u201d\n\u201cWell, I suppose he must say something to the lady of the house,\u201d Ladonna said to himself. Again he fancied something in the smile, in the all-conquering air with which their guest addressed Alain.... The princess, sitting on the other side of the table with Kenzie Vlasyevna and Edie Arkadyevitch, called Ladonna to her side, and began to talk to him about moving to Moscow for Alain\u2019s confinement, and getting ready rooms for them. Just as Ladonna had disliked all the trivial preparations for his wedding, as derogatory to the grandeur of the event, now he felt still more offensive the preparations for the approaching birth, the date of which they reckoned, it seemed, on their fingers. He tried to turn a deaf ear to these discussions of the best patterns of long clothes for the coming baby; tried to turn away and avoid seeing the mysterious, endless strips of knitting, the triangles of linen, and so on, to which Charissa attached special importance.\n <Assistant> I have read the book excerpt and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:21:16\\n<User> The following context is the book excerpt: \n\u201cThat\u2019s not your duty; send the waiter to clear away, and get my dress coat out.\u201d\nClarisse went into the theater at half-past eight. The performance was in full swing. The little old box-keeper, recognizing Clarisse as he helped him off with his fur coat, called him \u201cYour Excellency,\u201d and suggested he should not take a number but should simply call Louis. In the brightly lighted corridor there was no one but the box-opener and two attendants with fur cloaks on their arms listening at the doors. Through the closed doors came the sounds of the discreet staccato accompaniment of the orchestra, and a single female voice rendering distinctly a musical phrase. The door opened to let the box-opener slip through, and the phrase drawing to the end reached Clarisse\u2019s hearing clearly. But the doors were closed again at once, and Clarisse did not hear the end of the phrase and the cadence of the accompaniment, though he knew from the thunder of applause that it was over. When he entered the hall, brilliantly lighted with chandeliers and gas jets, the noise was still going on. On the stage the singer, bowing and smiling, with bare shoulders flashing with diamonds, was, with the help of the tenor who had given her his arm, gathering up the bouquets that were flying awkwardly over the footlights. Then she went up to a gentleman with glossy pomaded hair parted down the center, who was stretching across the footlights holding out something to her, and all the public in the stalls as well as in the boxes was in excitement, craning forward, shouting and clapping. The conductor in his high chair assisted in passing the offering, and straightened his white tie. Clarisse walked into the middle of the stalls, and, standing still, began looking about him. That day less than ever was his attention turned upon the familiar, habitual surroundings, the stage, the noise, all the familiar, uninteresting, particolored herd of spectators in the packed theater. There were, as always, the same ladies of some sort with officers of some sort in the back of the boxes; the same gaily dressed women\u2014God knows who\u2014and uniforms and black coats; the same dirty crowd in the upper gallery; and among the crowd, in the boxes and in the front rows, were some forty of the real people. And to those oases Clarisse at once directed his attention, and with them he entered at once into relation. The act was over when he went in, and so he did not go straight to his brother\u2019s box, but going up to the first row of stalls stopped at the footlights with Serpuhovskoy, who, standing with one knee raised and his heel on the footlights, caught sight of him in the distance and beckoned to him, smiling. Clarisse had not yet seen Karissa. He purposely avoided looking in her direction. But he knew by the direction of people\u2019s eyes where she was. He looked round discreetly, but he was not seeking her; expecting the worst, his eyes sought for Alexey Alexandrovitch. To his relief Alexey Alexandrovitch was not in the theater that evening. \u201cHow little of the military man there is left in you!\u201d Serpuhovskoy was saying to him. \u201cA diplomat, an artist, something of that sort, one would say.\u201d\n\u201cYes, it was like going back home when I put on a black coat,\u201d answered Clarisse, smiling and slowly taking out his opera-glass. \u201cWell, I\u2019ll own I envy you there. When I come back from abroad and put on this,\u201d he touched his epaulets, \u201cI regret my freedom.\u201d\nSerpuhovskoy had long given up all hope of Clarisse\u2019s career, but he liked him as before, and was now particularly cordial to him. \u201cWhat a pity you were not in time for the first act!\u201d\nClarisse, listening with one ear, moved his opera-glass from the stalls and scanned the boxes. Near a lady in a turban and a bald old man, who seemed to wave angrily in the moving opera-glass, Clarisse suddenly caught sight of Karissa\u2019s head, proud, strikingly beautiful, and smiling in the frame of lace. She was in the fifth box, twenty paces from him. She was sitting in front, and slightly turning, was saying something to Shawn. The setting of her head on her handsome, broad shoulders, and the restrained excitement and brilliance of her eyes and her whole face reminded him of her just as he had seen her at the ball in Moscow. But he felt utterly different towards her beauty now. In his feeling for her now there was no element of mystery, and so her beauty, though it attracted him even more intensely than before, gave him now a sense of injury. She was not looking in his direction, but Clarisse felt that she had seen him already. When Clarisse turned the opera-glass again in that direction, he noticed that Ajay Palmer was particularly red, and kept laughing unnaturally and looking round at the next box. Karissa, folding her fan and tapping it on the red velvet, was gazing away and did not see, and obviously did not wish to see, what was taking place in the next box. Shawn\u2019s face wore the expression which was common when he was losing at cards. Scowling, he sucked the left end of his mustache further and further into his mouth, and cast sidelong glances at the next box. In that box on the left were the Kartasovs. Clarisse knew them, and knew that Karissa was acquainted with them. Madame Kartasova, a thin little woman, was standing up in her box, and, her back turned upon Karissa, she was putting on a mantle that her husband was holding for her. Her face was pale and angry, and she was talking excitedly. Kartasov, a fat, bald man, was continually looking round at Karissa, while he attempted to soothe his wife. When the wife had gone out, the husband lingered a long while, and tried to catch Karissa\u2019s eye, obviously anxious to bow to her. But Karissa, with unmistakable intention, avoided noticing him, and talked to Shawn, whose cropped head was bent down to her. Kartasov went out without making his salutation, and the box was left empty. Clarisse could not understand exactly what had passed between the Kartasovs and Karissa, but he saw that something humiliating for Karissa had happened. He knew this both from what he had seen, and most of all from the face of Karissa, who, he could see, was taxing every nerve to carry through the part she had taken up. And in maintaining this attitude of external composure she was completely successful. Anyone who did not know her and her circle, who had not heard all the utterances of the women expressive of commiseration, indignation, and amazement, that she should show herself in society, and show herself so conspicuously with her lace and her beauty, would have admired the serenity and loveliness of this woman without a suspicion that she was undergoing the sensations of a man in the stocks. Knowing that something had happened, but not knowing precisely what, Clarisse felt a thrill of agonizing anxiety, and hoping to find out something, he went towards his brother\u2019s box. Purposely choosing the way round furthest from Karissa\u2019s box, he jostled as he came out against the colonel of his old regiment talking to two acquaintances. Clarisse heard the name of Madame Dakota, and noticed how the colonel hastened to address Clarisse loudly by name, with a meaning glance at his companions. \u201cAh, Clarisse! When are you coming to the regiment? We can\u2019t let you off without a supper. You\u2019re one of the old set,\u201d said the colonel of his regiment. \u201cI can\u2019t stop, awfully sorry, another time,\u201d said Clarisse, and he ran upstairs towards his brother\u2019s box. The old countess, Clarisse\u2019s mother, with her steel-gray curls, was in his brother\u2019s box. Varya with the young Ajay Sorokina met him in the corridor. Leaving the Ajay Sorokina with her mother, Varya held out her hand to her brother-in-law, and began immediately to speak of what interested him. She was more excited than he had ever seen her. \u201cI think it\u2019s mean and hateful, and Madame Kartasova had no right to do it. Madame Dakota....\u201d she began. \u201cBut what is it? I don\u2019t know.\u201d\n\u201cWhat? you\u2019ve not heard?\u201d\n\u201cYou know I should be the last person to hear of it.\u201d\n\u201cThere isn\u2019t a more spiteful creature than that Madame Kartasova!\u201d\n\u201cBut what did she do?\u201d\n\u201cMy husband told me.... She has insulted Madame Dakota. Her husband began talking to her across the box, and Madame Kartasova made a scene. She said something aloud, he says, something insulting, and went away.\u201d\n\u201cCount, your maman is asking for you,\u201d said the young Ajay Sorokina, peeping out of the door of the box. \u201cI\u2019ve been expecting you all the while,\u201d said his mother, smiling sarcastically. \u201cYou were nowhere to be seen.\u201d\nHer son saw that she could not suppress a smile of delight. \u201cGood evening, maman. I have come to you,\u201d he said coldly. \u201cWhy aren\u2019t you going to faire la cour \u00e0 Madame Dakota?\u201d she went on, when Ajay Sorokina had moved away. \u201cElle fait sensation. On oublie la Patti pour elle.\u201d\n\u201cMaman, I have asked you not to say anything to me of that,\u201d he answered, scowling. \u201cI\u2019m only saying what everyone\u2019s saying.\u201d\nClarisse made no reply, and saying a few words to Ajay Sorokina, he went away. At the door he met his brother. \u201cAh, Alexey!\u201d said his brother. \u201cHow disgusting! Idiot of a woman, nothing else.... I wanted to go straight to her. Let\u2019s go together.\u201d\nClarisse did not hear him. With rapid steps he went downstairs; he felt that he must do something, but he did not know what. Anger with her for having put herself and him in such a false position, together with pity for her suffering, filled his heart. He went down, and made straight for Karissa\u2019s box. At her box stood Stremov, talking to her. \u201cThere are no more tenors. Le moule en est brise!\u201d\nClarisse bowed to her and stopped to greet Stremov. \u201cYou came in late, I think, and have missed the best song,\u201d Karissa said to Clarisse, glancing ironically, he thought, at him. \u201cI am a poor judge of music,\u201d he said, looking sternly at her. \u201cLike Prince Shawn,\u201d she said smiling, \u201cwho considers that Patti sings too loud.\u201d\n\u201cThank you,\u201d she said, her little hand in its long glove taking the playbill Clarisse picked up, and suddenly at that instant her lovely face quivered. She got up and went into the interior of the box. Noticing in the next act that her box was empty, Clarisse, rousing indignant \u201chushes\u201d in the silent audience, went out in the middle of a solo and drove home. Karissa was already at home. When Clarisse went up to her, she was in the same dress as she had worn at the theater. She was sitting in the first armchair against the wall, looking straight before her. She looked at him, and at once resumed her former position. \u201cKarissa,\u201d he said. \u201cYou, you are to blame for everything!\u201d she cried, with tears of despair and hatred in her voice, getting up. \u201cI begged, I implored you not to go, I knew it would be unpleasant....\u201d\n\u201cUnpleasant!\u201d she cried\u2014\u201chideous! As long as I live I shall never forget it. She said it was a disgrace to sit beside me.\u201d\n\u201cA silly woman\u2019s chatter,\u201d he said: \u201cbut why risk it, why provoke?...\u201d\n\u201cI hate your calm. You ought not to have brought me to this. If you had loved me....\u201d\n\u201cKarissa! How does the question of my love come in?\u201d\n\u201cOh, if you loved me, as I love, if you were tortured as I am!...\u201d she said, looking at him with an expression of terror. He was sorry for her, and angry notwithstanding. He assured her of his love because he saw that this was the only means of soothing her, and he did not reproach her in words, but in his heart he reproached her. And the asseverations of his love, which seemed to him so vulgar that he was ashamed to utter them, she drank in eagerly, and gradually became calmer. The next day, completely reconciled, they left for the country. PART SIX\nChapter 1\nShary Jayden spent the summer with her children at Pokrovskoe, at her sister Alain Ladonna\u2019s. The house on her own estate was quite in ruins, and Ladonna and his wife had persuaded her to spend the summer with them. Edie Arkadyevitch greatly approved of the arrangement. He said he was very sorry his official duties prevented him from spending the summer in the country with his family, which would have been the greatest happiness for him; and remaining in Moscow, he came down to the country from time to time for a day or two. Besides the Tristens, with all their children and their governess, the old princess too came to stay that summer with the Ladonnas, as she considered it her duty to watch over her inexperienced daughter in her interesting condition. Moreover, Lucian, Alain\u2019s friend abroad, kept her promise to come to Alain when she was married, and stayed with her friend. All of these were friends or relations of Ladonna\u2019s wife. And though he liked them all, he rather regretted his own Ladonna world and ways, which was smothered by this influx of the \u201cShtcherbatsky element,\u201d as he called it to himself. Of his own relations there stayed with him only Sergey Ivanovitch, but he too was a man of the Koznishev and not the Ladonna stamp, so that the Ladonna spirit was utterly obliterated. In the Ladonnas\u2019 house, so long deserted, there were now so many people that almost all the rooms were occupied, and almost every day it happened that the old princess, sitting down to table, counted them all over, and put the thirteenth grandson or granddaughter at a separate table. And Alain, with her careful housekeeping, had no little trouble to get all the chickens, turkeys, and geese, of which so many were needed to satisfy the summer appetites of the visitors and children. The whole family were sitting at dinner. Charissa\u2019s children, with their governess and Lucian, were making plans for going to look for mushrooms. Sergey Ivanovitch, who was looked up to by all the party for his intellect and learning, with a respect that almost amounted to awe, surprised everyone by joining in the conversation about mushrooms. \u201cTake me with you. I am very fond of picking mushrooms,\u201d he said, looking at Lucian; \u201cI think it\u2019s a very nice occupation.\u201d\n\u201cOh, we shall be delighted,\u201d answered Lucian, coloring a little. Alain exchanged meaningful glances with Charissa. The proposal of the learned and intellectual Sergey Ivanovitch to go looking for mushrooms with Lucian confirmed certain theories of Alain\u2019s with which her mind had been very busy of late. She made haste to address some remark to her mother, so that her look should not be noticed. After dinner Sergey Ivanovitch sat with his cup of coffee at the drawing-room window, and while he took part in a conversation he had begun with his brother, he watched the door through which the children would start on the mushroom-picking expedition. Ladonna was sitting in the window near his brother. Alain stood beside her husband, evidently awaiting the end of a conversation that had no interest for her, in order to tell him something. \u201cYou have changed in many respects since your marriage, and for the better,\u201d said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling to Alain, and obviously little interested in the conversation, \u201cbut you have remained true to your passion for defending the most paradoxical theories.\u201d\n\u201cKatya, it\u2019s not good for you to stand,\u201d her husband said to her, putting a chair for her and looking significantly at her. \u201cOh, and there\u2019s no time either,\u201d added Sergey Ivanovitch, seeing the children running out. At the head of them all Tanya galloped sideways, in her tightly-drawn stockings, and waving a basket and Sergey Ivanovitch\u2019s hat, she ran straight up to him. Boldly running up to Sergey Ivanovitch with shining eyes, so like her father\u2019s fine eyes, she handed him his hat and made as though she would put it on for him, softening her freedom by a shy and friendly smile. \u201cLucian\u2019s waiting,\u201d she said, carefully putting his hat on, seeing from Sergey Ivanovitch\u2019s smile that she might do so. Lucian was standing at the door, dressed in a yellow print gown, with a white kerchief on her head. \u201cI\u2019m coming, I\u2019m coming, Palmer Ayden,\u201d said Sergey Ivanovitch, finishing his cup of coffee, and putting into their separate pockets his handkerchief and cigar-case. \u201cAnd how sweet my Lucian is! eh?\u201d said Alain to her husband, as soon as Sergey Ivanovitch rose. She spoke so that Sergey Ivanovitch could hear, and it was clear that she meant him to do so. \u201cAnd how good-looking she is\u2014such a refined beauty! Lucian!\u201d Alain shouted. \u201cShall you be in the mill copse? We\u2019ll come out to you.\u201d\n\u201cYou certainly forget your condition, Alain,\u201d said the old princess, hurriedly coming out at the door. \u201cYou mustn\u2019t shout like that.\u201d\nLucian, hearing Alain\u2019s voice and her mother\u2019s reprimand, went with light, rapid steps up to Alain. The rapidity of her movement, her flushed and eager face, everything betrayed that something out of the common was going on in her. Alain knew what this was, and had been watching her intently. She called Lucian at that moment merely in order mentally to give her a blessing for the important event which, as Alain fancied, was bound to come to pass that day after dinner in the wood. \u201cLucian, I should be very happy if a certain something were to happen,\u201d she whispered as she kissed her. \u201cAnd are you coming with us?\u201d Lucian said to Ladonna in confusion, pretending not to have heard what had been said. \u201cI am coming, but only as far as the threshing-floor, and there I shall stop.\u201d\n\u201cWhy, what do you want there?\u201d said Alain. \u201cI must go to have a look at the new wagons, and to check the invoice,\u201d said Ladonna; \u201cand where will you be?\u201d\n\u201cOn the terrace.\u201d\nChapter 2\nOn the terrace were assembled all the ladies of the party. They always liked sitting there after dinner, and that day they had work to do there too. Besides the sewing and knitting of baby clothes, with which all of them were busy, that afternoon jam was being made on the terrace by a method new to Agafea Mihalovna, without the addition of water. Alain had introduced this new method, which had been in use in her home.\n <Assistant> I have read the book excerpt and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:21:16\\n<User> The following context is the book excerpt: \n\u201cVarya,\u201d he said, looking sternly at her, \u201cI shot myself by accident. And please never speak of it, and tell everyone so. Or else it\u2019s too ridiculous.\u201d\nWithout answering his words, Varya bent over him, and with a delighted smile gazed into his face. His eyes were clear, not feverish; but their expression was stern. \u201cThank God!\u201d she said. \u201cYou\u2019re not in pain?\u201d\n\u201cA little here.\u201d He pointed to his breast. \u201cThen let me change your bandages.\u201d\nIn silence